---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TechnicalPlate from "../../components/TechnicalPlate.astro";
import D2Diagram from "../../components/D2Diagram.astro";
export const prerender = false;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Factory doctrine"
  description="The operating doctrine for the Loftwah Software Factory: move quickly while mistakes are cheap, add controls as value and consequence rise."
  path="/doctrine/"
  image="/og/factory.jpg"
>
  <section class="container page-intro">
    <div class="page-intro__grid">
      <div>
        <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <a href="/">WOODHOUSE</a>
          <span>/</span>
          <span>Doctrine</span>
        </nav>
        <h1 class="page-title">Authority follows the cost of a mistake.</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="page-intro__aside">
        <strong>Move quickly while mistakes are cheap.</strong>Add controls as value and consequence
        rise. A capable agent is not an unlimited authority.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <h2>Process should scale with what can be lost.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          A throwaway experiment should not carry commercial process. Customer money, private data,
          valuable work and physical safety change the required evidence.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="container container--reading prose">
        <div class="pullquote">
          Autonomy should scale with reversibility. Governance should scale with value and
          consequence.<cite>Core doctrine</cite>
        </div>
      </div>
      <TechnicalPlate
        id="value-classes"
        number="02"
        title="Value classes and controls"
        variant="ladder"
        diagram="risk-controls"
        note="Controls increase with value, irreversibility and consequence. Classification is a working judgement, not an automatic risk score."
        steps={[
          { label: "Class 00", title: "Throwaway", detail: "Explore freely; discard cheaply." },
          {
            label: "Class 01",
            title: "Experiment",
            detail: "Preserve learning; add useful checks."
          },
          {
            label: "Class 02",
            title: "Project",
            detail: "Protect source, repeatability and records."
          },
          {
            label: "Class 03",
            title: "Product",
            detail: "Prove users, access and production quality."
          },
          {
            label: "Class 04",
            title: "Commercial",
            detail: "Control money, promises and rollback."
          },
          {
            label: "Class 05",
            title: "Treasured",
            detail: "Narrow authority; prove recovery and safety."
          }
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container container--reading prose">
      <h2>Agents do the routine work. MP is not the default planner.</h2>
      <p>
        Most of this factory is agents reasoning. MP is the human principal and the final authority,
        but not the default planner, memory store, specification writer or decision engine. An agent
        that brings MP a choice between two defensible options has handed back work it should have
        finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        What genuinely needs MP is a capability an agent does not have: taste, physical hardware,
        money, credentials, external accounts, legal judgement, an irreversible action, or a gate
        this factory has deliberately reserved. Everything else is the agent's work, carried to
        completion and shown rather than proposed.
      </p>
      <h3>The default loop</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Reconstruct current truth from the repository and the running product.</li>
        <li>
          Treat the issue as a claim about a desired outcome, not as proof that the proposed fix or
          the existing test is correct.
        </li>
        <li>For user-facing work, inspect or play the product before changing it.</li>
        <li>
          Find the smallest material discrepancy blocking the outcome, and take it to completion.
        </li>
        <li>
          Validate at the cheapest meaningful level, then at the integrated level its risk earns.
        </li>
        <li>
          If a gate conflicts with current product intent, investigate the gate before changing the
          product to satisfy it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Re-inspect the owning outcome after integration. If it is still incomplete, leave the
          parent open and name the next real discrepancy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="pullquote">
        Done means the observable outcome exists at the authority level the project claims. Making
        the change is not the same thing as finishing the work.<cite>Core doctrine</cite>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container container--reading prose">
      <h2>A prompt can end with a real object.</h2>
      <p>
        The factory can compose software with a payment provider, a printer, a manufacturer, a
        contractor or a person doing work that software cannot. MAX makes the boundary obvious: a
        working PWA is not a real walker. The hardware, mounting, microphone, speaker, lights and
        the person's ability to use FIND still need physical proof.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same route could take approved artwork to a poster, shirt or book through a fulfilment
        provider such as Printful. Agents can prepare the files, product details and order workflow.
        A provider makes and ships the object. The factory records what happened and what still
        needs a person to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        An eligible event could ask an AI system to draft artwork, copy, a diagram or a product
        variant. That output stays a candidate. Check the brief, rights, visual quality, print
        dimensions and cost, then ask Dean to approve, return or reject it before it becomes a
        listing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before work crosses that boundary, confirm that it is legal, the assets may be used, the
        provider is authorised, the budget is available, the result is safe and the outcome can be
        verified. If any of those answers is missing, stop at a proposal.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <h2>Events and schedules can start work. They cannot approve it.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          A milestone, failed check, shipment event or scheduled review can ask an agent to prepare
          the next change. Production access starts read-only.
        </p>
      </div>
      <D2Diagram
        title="Work stops at the human gate"
        description="Project events, scheduled checks and read-only production observations create an agent proposal. Policy and qualification run before Dean reviews that exact candidate and approves, returns or rejects it."
        desktop="/images/diagrams/agent-policy.svg"
        mobile="/images/diagrams/agent-policy-mobile.svg"
        caption="An approval is tied to one qualified candidate. A revised candidate goes through the checks again."
        className="agent-policy-plate"
      >
        <ol class="diagram-transcript">
          <li>
            <strong>Event or scheduled review</strong>
            <span>
              A release, failed check, order, shipment or recurring audit may start a bounded task.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Read-only production</strong>
            <span>
              Agents gather permitted evidence. They do not turn an observation into a production
              write.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Proposal</strong>
            <span>
              The output is a draft, issue or changeset with evidence, rationale, risk and rollback.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Policy and qualification</strong>
            <span>
              Check scope, asset rights, spend, required gates and recovery before asking for a
              decision.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Dean decides</strong>
            <span>
              Approve the qualified candidate, return it for changes or reject it. A new revision
              needs a new review.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </D2Diagram>
      <div class="container--reading prose">
        <p>
          Scheduled improvement works the same way. A check can spot an old dependency, stale
          screenshot, broken journey or drifting document. It creates a proposal or work item. It
          does not silently edit or deploy production.
        </p>
        <p>
          Project value sets the amount of process. A throwaway experiment stays cheap to change.
          Customer money, promises, private data, hard-to-replace work or physical safety add
          controls when a project earns them. A repository count or an AI-generated market estimate
          does not make something a company. Record actual customers, revenue, use, replacement
          cost, creative value and operating risk separately.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <h2>A payment event must lead to one controlled fulfilment.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>
          A browser redirect cannot settle an order. Payment state comes from the payment provider,
          then a durable workflow records the fulfilment that follows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <D2Diagram
        title="One paid order, one fulfilment path"
        description="Dean approves the catalogue item, artwork rights, price and spend limits. The customer pays through a payment provider. A validated, deduplicated event updates the order ledger and may create one Printful order within those limits. Shipment events update the same record."
        desktop="/images/diagrams/payment-fulfilment.svg"
        mobile="/images/diagrams/payment-fulfilment-mobile.svg"
        caption="Retries and duplicate events must not create a second charge, order, shipment or refund."
        className="payment-fulfilment-plate"
      >
        <ol class="diagram-transcript">
          <li>
            <strong>Approve the catalogue</strong>
            <span>
              Dean approves artwork, rights, price, provider and per-order spend limits before a
              product is offered.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Confirm payment from its source</strong>
            <span>
              Verify the webhook signature, payment status, amount and order. A return to a success
              URL is not proof of payment.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Deduplicate before acting</strong>
            <span>
              Record stable event and order IDs. Use idempotent provider requests so a retry cannot
              make a second order.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fulfil within policy</strong>
            <span>
              Send Printful an approved order only when the item and spend fit the published limits.
              Hold exceptions for Dean.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Follow the shipment</strong>
            <span>
              Provider events update the order record and customer notification. Physical product
              quality still needs a real check.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </D2Diagram>
      <div class="container container--reading prose">
        <p>
          A commercial pipeline can automate orders inside limits Dean approved in advance. A new
          product, unusual amount, refund, supplier purchase or other exception waits for a
          decision. The approval is durable and records whether Dean approved it, returned it with
          notes or rejected it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Validate events, make handlers idempotent and keep an order ledger. A retry must not
          charge twice, buy two shirts, send duplicate refunds or tell a customer that an unshipped
          order shipped. Payment details stay with the payment provider; Woodhouse's order record
          needs the provider's event and transaction references, not card numbers.
        </p>
        <p class="source-note">
          <strong>Technical references:</strong>{" "}
          <a
            href="https://docs.stripe.com/payments/existing-customers?platform=web&amp;ui=stripe-hosted"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Stripe payment events
          </a>{" "}
          ·{" "}
          <a
            href="https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Stripe idempotency
          </a>{" "}
          ·{" "}
          <a href="https://developers.printful.com/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Printful orders and webhooks
          </a>{" "}
          ·{" "}
          <a
            href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/concepts/agentic-patterns/human-in-the-loop/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Cloudflare human approval workflows
          </a>{" "}
          ·{" "}
          <a
            href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/agents/runtime/execution/schedule-tasks/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            Cloudflare scheduled agent tasks
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <p class="source-note">
          <strong>Scope:</strong> Woodhouse publishes these rules. This release does not process
          payments, call Printful, schedule agent work or write to production.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div class="split-section__copy">
        <h2>Observe more than you mutate.</h2>
        <p>
          Agents can collect production evidence and prepare a change. Before a consequential
          action, the change needs a reviewable diff, a reason, qualification and a rollback path.
          The approval is tied to that exact candidate and cannot be skipped by asking again in
          chat.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-column-list">
        <article class="method-step">
          <span class="method-step__n">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Observe</h3>
            <p>Read permitted state and source material.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="method-step">
          <span class="method-step__n">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Recommend</h3>
            <p>Explain what changed and what remains uncertain.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="method-step">
          <span class="method-step__n">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Prepare</h3>
            <p>Produce a reviewable change with evidence and rollback.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="method-step">
          <span class="method-step__n">04</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Execute by policy</h3>
            <p>
              Bounded work may proceed inside approved limits. New financial, destructive or
              physical actions wait for a human decision.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--reading prose">
      <h2>Failures teach the factory.</h2>
      <p>
        Contain the failure. Record it as an incident or tracked defect, attach the evidence, and
        investigate which control missed it. Link the work item to the changeset, qualification and
        production follow-up. Then decide whether the factory needs a regression check, release
        gate, policy, architecture or documentation change.
      </p>
      <p>
        A recurring failure should become harder to repeat because the factory learned from it.
        Public claims remain smaller than the evidence supporting them.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        Give agents room to work where mistakes are cheap. Add review where a mistake can cost
        users, money, data or safety.<cite>Core doctrine</cite>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container container--reading prose">
      <h2>A gate is evidence for a requirement, not the requirement.</h2>
      <p>
        Checks accumulate. Some outlive the behaviour they were written to protect, and a factory
        that treats an outdated gate as a specification will warp a correct product to satisfy a
        test that no longer describes anything real.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a gate and current product intent disagree, investigate the gate first. Ask whether the
        requirement still exists. If it does not, delete or rewrite the gate and record what it used
        to protect. If it does, the product was wrong. What is not acceptable is deleting the
        requirement quietly to make the disagreement disappear.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same bar applies to process work itself. New process, tooling or architecture needs a
        named observed failure behind it. A control added because a check could not actually run
        against production, or because one worktree could certify another, has earned its place. A
        control added because it seemed prudent is a cost with no evidence attached.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section paper-section">
    <div class="container container--reading prose">
      <h2>Spend against evidence, not against possibility.</h2>
      <p>
        Money is the one input in this factory that cannot be un-spent, and the temptation it
        creates is specific: a capability that could be paid for feels available, and availability
        is easy to mistake for readiness. The rule is a sequence, not a mood.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Preserve the option. Keeping a path open is cheap and keeps the choice real.</li>
        <li>Validate cheaply. Find the smallest experiment that could be wrong.</li>
        <li>Increase spending only as evidence increases.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Applied honestly, this rules out a whole category of expensive mistake. Capital is not
        wasted by waiting for a technology that makes a project possible; it is wasted by spending
        early to find out whether a market exists. A factory that can already build the system has
        demonstrated one thing: it can build systems. That says nothing about whether strangers will
        pay for them, and the two claims are routinely confused because both feel like progress.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technical maturity and commercial validation are separate claims with separate evidence. A
        deployed product, a passing suite and a growing catalogue are implementation, release and
        production verification. None of them is a customer. Where the only honest measurement is an
        empty table — the tables that would hold a rating, a report or a dispute, with no rows —
        that is a measurement worth publishing rather than a gap to route around.
      </p>
      <p>
        This also constrains the negative direction. Creative work is allowed to be worth doing
        without a business attached, and a project with no commercial ambition is not a project
        awaiting justification. Forcing a revenue thesis onto work that has none produces a worse
        answer than leaving it alone, and manufacturing the appearance of demand is worse than
        recording its absence.
      </p>
      <div class="pullquote">
        Preserve optionality, validate cheaply, and increase spending only as evidence increases.
        <cite>Core doctrine</cite>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
